# Planning Behavior

Apply these rules whenever preparing an implementation plan.

This document governs planning behavior only.

---

# Planning objective

The purpose of planning is to understand the existing system and produce an implementation plan that can be executed safely.

Planning is an investigation.

Planning is not implementation.

Planning is not architecture design.

Do not modify code during planning.

---

# Sources of truth

Always investigate in the following order:

1. Current TASK document.
2. Approved architecture documentation.
3. Existing production implementation.
4. Backend contracts.
5. Existing tests.

Do not invent missing behavior while planning.

If authoritative sources conflict:

- identify the conflict;
- explain it;
- stop and request clarification.

---

# Repository exploration

Read only what is required.

Always begin with:

- AI_CONTEXT.md
- current TASK
- directly related architecture documents

Then locate:

- closest existing feature
- closest existing API
- closest existing tests
- closest existing UI pattern

Do not scan unrelated modules.

Prefer targeted search over broad repository exploration.

---

# Existing implementation first

Never assume a feature should be implemented from scratch.

Before proposing a solution:

- find similar implementations;
- reuse existing architecture;
- reuse existing patterns;
- reuse shared components;
- reuse validation;
- reuse testing strategy.

New patterns require explicit justification.

---

# Backend contracts

Treat backend contracts as authoritative.

Do not invent:

- endpoints;
- permissions;
- lifecycle transitions;
- DTO fields;
- filters;
- sorting;
- calculations.

If the backend does not expose something:

- document the limitation;
- do not design replacement APIs.

---

# Architecture boundaries

Respect existing boundaries.

Do not plan:

- cross-feature internal imports;
- duplicated shared components;
- duplicated utilities;
- duplicated DTOs;
- duplicated business logic.

Prefer public feature boundaries.

Prefer shared abstractions only when they already exist.

---

# Scope discipline

Plan only the requested task.

Do not:

- add future work;
- redesign unrelated modules;
- refactor unrelated code;
- introduce speculative abstractions.

Every planned activity must be traceable to:

- TASK
or
- approved architecture.

---

# Phase planning

Large implementations must be divided into implementation phases.

Each phase should:

- have a clear objective;
- be independently testable;
- be independently reviewable;
- minimize implementation risk.

Avoid phases that modify unrelated areas simultaneously.

Prefer vertical slices over large horizontal changes.

---

# Required planning output

Every implementation plan should include:

## Goal

Implementation objective.

---

## Approved assumptions

Assumptions confirmed by repository evidence.

---

## Open questions

Items requiring clarification before implementation.

---

## Backend contract

Relevant:

- endpoints
- DTOs
- permissions
- lifecycle
- validation

---

## Architecture boundaries

Allowed dependencies.

Forbidden dependencies.

Shared components to reuse.

---

## Affected files

Only files expected to change.

---

## Implementation phases

Small, reviewable phases.

Each phase should define:

- scope;
- excluded work;
- expected verification.

---

## Acceptance Criteria coverage

Map implementation phases to Acceptance Criteria.

Every Acceptance Criterion should be covered.

---

## Verification strategy

Focused verification for each phase.

Include:

- unit tests;
- integration tests;
- E2E where applicable;
- type checking;
- linting when required.

---

## Risks

Identify:

- contract risks;
- architecture risks;
- migration risks;
- testing risks.

Do not speculate beyond repository evidence.

---

## Known limitations

Document backend limitations instead of inventing functionality.

---

# Planning quality

A good plan:

- minimizes implementation risk;
- minimizes repository changes;
- minimizes future rework;
- maximizes reuse;
- clearly separates implementation phases.

---

# Before implementation

Before implementation begins, verify:

- implementation plan exists;
- plan has been reviewed;
- plan has been approved;
- implementation phases are defined;
- architecture boundaries are understood;
- backend contract has been confirmed.

Do not begin implementation before explicit approval.

---

# Reporting

At the end of planning, report:

- documents inspected;
- implementation patterns reused;
- backend contracts identified;
- affected files;
- implementation phases;
- verification strategy;
- risks;
- open questions.

Do not report implementation progress.

Planning ends when an implementation-ready plan has been approved.

---

# Core principle

Planning discovers the existing system.

It does not invent a new one.